-- #1326 REGRESSION GUARD (Repro A). Two modules export the same bare name `h1326`, one
-- with a `Display a =>` context and one with none. Importing both -- the constrained one
-- under an alias, the unconstrained one plainly -- and calling the UNCONSTRAINED one on a
-- type with no `Display` impl was rejected at that call site with the OTHER module's
-- obligation. A legal program refused.
--
-- ⚠️ The load-bearing call is `tag (h1326 NoDisp)`: `NoDisp` deliberately has NO `Display`
-- impl, so the fixture fails the instant the constrained sibling's context is attributed
-- here. `ph 5` is present only to keep BOTH imports live -- each half alone was always
-- fine, and it is having both in scope that triggered the misattribution.
--
-- Guarded here as well as in test/import_order_fixtures/ because the two gates observe
-- different things: that one asserts the answer does not depend on clause ORDER, this one
-- asserts check/run/build agree AND pins the value both engines must print.
--
-- EXPECTED VALUE DERIVED BY HAND, not captured: `h1326` in scope here is qs's identity
-- function, so `tag (h1326 NoDisp)` is `tag NoDisp` = "ok"; `ph 5` interpolates 5 at
-- `Display Int` = "5".
import lib.obl1326_ps.{h1326 as ph}
import lib.obl1326_qs.{h1326}

data NoDisp = NoDisp

tag : NoDisp -> String
tag _ = "ok"

main = println "\{tag (h1326 NoDisp)} / \{ph 5}"
